Uncomplicated Cystitis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Dysuria, Urinary urgency and frequency, Suprapubic discomfort

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-045
Reason for Visit: Acute urinary burning and extreme frequency.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an explosive onset of severe dysuria and urinary urgency, currently necessitating voiding every 15 minutes. The patient describes significant suprapubic heaviness and is in visible distress. An urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to perform a physical evaluation, rule out ascending pyelonephritis (indicated by a lack of systemic toxicity or costovertebral angle tenderness), and provide immediate empiric antimicrobial therapy. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period poses an unacceptable risk of escalating pain, potential upper urinary tract involvement, and significant psychological distress, strictly fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: It feels like I'm passing shards of glass every time I go to the toilet, and I can't leave the bathroom.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 6 hours ago with mild suprapubic discomfort, rapidly progressing to severe dysuria characterized by a sharp burning sensation during micturition. The patient reports a dramatic increase in urinary frequency and a constant, urgent need to urinate even with an empty bladder. Notes the urine appears cloudy and malodorous. Denies vaginal discharge, irritation, or fever. No previous history of recurrent urinary tract infections reported.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently home-bound due to urinary urgency.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute distress.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.1 °C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Appears acutely uncomfortable, frequently shifting position.
Recent/Relevant Labs: Urine dipstick performed on-site is positive for leukocyte esterase and nitrites, assisting in confirming the diagnosis.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals localized tenderness and heaviness upon suprapubic palpation. Crucially, there is an absolute lack of costovertebral angle (CVA) tenderness bilaterally. No guarding, rigidity, or rebound tenderness noted. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are entirely normal.
Environmental Observations: Safe, standard residential environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient self-administered a single urinary alkalinizer sachet with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Uncomplicated Cystitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Pyelonephritis - Ruled out definitively by the lack of costovertebral angle tenderness, absence of high-grade fever, and absence of systemic toxicity.
Vulvovaginitis - Unlikely given the explicit absence of vaginal discharge or localized external irritation, with symptoms localized strictly to the urinary tract.
Urethritis - Less likely given the prominent suprapubic discomfort and clinical context, though remains a consideration if symptoms persist post-antibiotics.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient's acute, severe dysuria and frequency necessitated an urgent evaluation to exclude an ascending infection (pyelonephritis) and provide immediate relief. Physical examination confirmed localized bladder inflammation without upper tract involvement. The immediate provision of empiric antibiotics and targeted analgesia was required to arrest the infection and alleviate the debilitating symptoms that made standard travel to a clinic impossible. This stabilization could not safely wait until the next standard operating period, satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Nitrofurantoin 100mg twice daily for 5 days. Administered a urinary alkalinizer stat to provide symptomatic relief by neutralizing urine acidity.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically flush the bacteria out of your bladder, drink exactly 250ml of water every hour for the next 12 hours. This constant high-volume flow mechanically prevents bacteria from adhering to the bladder wall, significantly speeding up the effectiveness of the antibiotics.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 3-5 days if symptoms do not completely resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop severe back or flank pain, high fevers with shaking chills (rigors), or if you begin vomiting and are unable to keep your medication down.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on dipstick and symptoms; definitive culture results take 48-72 hours if sent.
Clinical Course: Burning during urination should begin to subside within 24 hours of starting antibiotics. Frequency will gradually normalize as bladder irritation settles over 2-3 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you notice blood in your urine that does not clear after the first few doses of antibiotics.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: Bacteria have entered the bladder and are irritating the lining, causing the "glass-like" pain and the false feeling of needing to pee constantly. The antibiotics kill the bacteria, while the alkalinizer neutralizes the acid in your urine so it doesn't sting the raw areas as much.
Actionable Steps: Complete the entire course of antibiotics even if you feel 100% better in two days. Always wipe from front to back after using the toilet to prevent bacteria from your digestive tract from entering the urinary system.

